.TH smi-incoming-deploy "1" "September 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Base Command"
.SH NAME
smi-incoming-deploy \- Hand incoming deployment packages to deployment plugins
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-incoming-deploy
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-incoming-deploy
processes deployment packages dropped into the incoming directories: for every
.B .tar.gz
file it runs each executable
.B .sh
plugin from
.I /opt/setmy.info/lib/incoming
with the package path and the stage as arguments, in the order of the plugin names.
.SH STAGES
.PP
A package is deployed into one of two stages, and the external system chooses by the directory it
drops the package into:
.PP
.RS
.nf
/var/opt/setmy.info/incoming/*.tar.gz         published
/var/opt/setmy.info/incoming/draft/*.tar.gz   draft
.fi
.RE
.PP
Both are deployed on the same machine, the live one; the stage is only how far the deployment has
got. A draft is checked by whoever approves it, and the external system publishes it by sending
the same package to
.IR /var/opt/setmy.info/incoming ,
or rejects it by sending nothing. Nothing on this machine approves or rejects.
.PP
The stage is the second argument every plugin is given,
.B published
or
.BR draft ,
so one plugin deploys both and decides from that argument where the files go.
A failed package is kept in the failed directory of its own stage,
.I /var/opt/setmy.info/failed
or
.IR /var/opt/setmy.info/failed/draft .
.PP
Drafts are deployed before the published packages of the same run, so a package that is in both
directories at once ends with the published one in place.
.PP
One package can have any number of handlers.
Every plugin is given every package and decides for itself whether the package is one of its own;
a plugin that is not interested ends with
.B 0
and does nothing.
What a package means is entirely up to the plugins.
.PP
A plugin does the work and says how it went with its exit status.
The package file belongs to this command, not to the plugins:
.IP \(bu 2
every plugin handled the package: the file is removed
.IP \(bu 2
a plugin ended with an error: the plugins after it are
.B not
run, and the file is moved to
.I /var/opt/setmy.info/failed
.PP
The first error therefore ends the handling of that package, so a half deployed package is kept
for inspection instead of being handed to the rest.
The name in the failed directory gets a UTC timestamp in front of it, so a package that fails
again does not overwrite the earlier one, and the reason is written to standard error, which
.BR journalctl (1)
keeps for the service.
Plugins run in the order of their names, which is also the order a failure cancels: a plugin that
sorts after the one that failed does not run.
.PP
The incoming directory is empty when the command ends.
Leaving a package in the incoming directory is what the
.B setmy-info-deploy.path
unit triggers on, so a package left there would start the deployment again and again, burning
processor time until systemd's start limit stopped it. That is why nothing is left behind.
.PP
A failing plugin ends the handling of its own package only: the packages after it in the incoming
directory are still processed, and the command exits with
.B 1
at the end so the service is recorded as failed.
.SH PLUGINS
.PP
The plugins shipped are symbolic links to one generic script,
.IR /opt/setmy.info/lib/smi-incoming-deploy.sh .
The script takes its pattern from the name it was started under, the link name without
.BR .sh ,
and handles a package only when the package file name contains that pattern; other packages are
ignored with success.
.PP
It deploys one application of one virtual host, and the stage decides which of the two
directories of that application is written:
.PP
.RS
.nf
published  /usr/share/nginx/vhosts/setmy.info.gintra/apps/old
draft      /usr/share/nginx/vhosts/setmy.info.gintra/draft/apps/old
.fi
.RE
.PP
The host and the application are the
.B VHOST
and
.B APP
values at the top of the script.
The virtual host directory has to exist already, because the nginx configuration decides which
names are served.
.PP
An archive whose files are under one top directory is unpacked without it, so an Angular build of
.I dist/old/index.html
becomes
.IR apps/old/index.html ;
an archive with its files in the root is unpacked as it is.
The files are unpacked into a directory beside the target and moved into place with one
.BR mv ,
with the previous deployment moved aside first and put back when the move fails, so a request is
served either the previous deployment or the new one and never a half unpacked directory.
Owner
.BR root:root ,
.B 0755
directories,
.B 0644
files and
.B restorecon
are applied after the move, because the SELinux type is inherited from the directory the files
were created in.
.PP
A draft deployment is therefore checked under the draft variant, and the external system
publishes it by sending the same package to
.IR /var/opt/setmy.info/incoming .
.PP
Links created by the rpm and deb install scripts, and removed again on uninstall:
.TP
.I angular-start-project.sh
Deploys every incoming package whose file name contains
.BR angular-start-project ,
for example
.IR angular-start-project-1.0.0.tar.gz ,
as the application the script names.
.PP
Another deployment is another link, named after the packages it handles, and a copy of the script
with its own
.B VHOST
and
.B APP
when it is another application:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ln -s ../smi-incoming-deploy.sh /opt/setmy.info/lib/incoming/my-site.sh
.fi
.RE
.PP
A pattern is matched anywhere in the file name, so a link named
.I shop.sh
also handles
.IR webshop-2.0.tar.gz ;
choose names that are not part of other package names.
.PP
Upload a package under a different name or directory and move it into the incoming directory when
it is complete: the watcher reacts to the file as soon as it exists, and a package that is still
being written fails to unpack.
.PP
The command is quiet by design: a missing incoming directory, an empty one, or an absent plugin
directory all end with success, so it is safe to run from cron or a timer.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/opt/setmy.info/incoming
Incoming packages, one
.B .tar.gz
per deployment.
.TP
.I /var/opt/setmy.info/incoming/draft
Draft packages, dropped there by the external system.
.TP
.I /var/opt/setmy.info/failed
.TQ
.I /var/opt/setmy.info/failed/draft
Packages that were not deployed, next to the incoming directory, one directory per stage, each
package with a UTC timestamp in front of its name.
.TP
.I /opt/setmy.info/lib/incoming
Deployment plugins, executable
.B .sh
files; the shipped ones are links to
.IR smi-incoming-deploy.sh .
.TP
.I /opt/setmy.info/lib/smi-incoming-deploy.sh
Generic nginx deployment plugin, selected by the name of the link it is started through.
.TP
.I /usr/share/nginx/vhosts/HOST
Virtual host directory, with
.IR apps/APP ,
.I brand
and the
.I draft
variant of both.
.TP
.I /usr/share/nginx/vhosts/HOST/apps/APP
.TQ
.I /usr/share/nginx/vhosts/HOST/draft/apps/APP
The published and the draft directory of one application, what the plugin writes.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every plugin handled every package and the files were removed, or there was nothing to do.
.TP
.B 1
A plugin ended with an error and its package was moved to the failed directory, or a package
could not be removed from or moved out of the incoming directory.
The packages after it were still processed.
